This advice also applies when http/2 is disabled. In that case `bindAndHandleAsync` binds `Flow.mapAsync(handler)` through `bindAndHandleImpl` -> `bindAndHandle`, so the handler wrapper now ends up nested inside `PekkoFlowWrapper`. `PekkoFlowWrapper` removes `PekkoTracingRequest.ATTR_KEY` before pushing the request downstream, so `PekkoHttpServerHandlerWrapper.apply` never sees the traced request and falls through to the "start a new span" branch.

Today that is harmless only because the `GraphInterpreterInstrumentation` push advice has already made the server context current, so `instrumenter().shouldStart(...)` returns false via span suppression. With `otel.instrumentation.experimental.span-suppression-strategy=none`, every plain http/1.1 request (on all pekko-http versions, not just 1.1) would get a second, nested SERVER span. Before this change the handler wrapper was never on that path.

Only pekko-http 1.1.x inlines the http/2 binding (1.0, 1.2, 1.3 and 1.4 all delegate to `Http2Ext.bindAndHandleAsync`), so the new advice is needed, but I think the wrapper should pass through http/1.1 requests that don't carry the tracing attribute, e.g. in `PekkoHttpServerHandlerWrapper.apply`:

```java
if (tracedRequest == null && !isHttp2(serverRequest)) {
  // already traced by PekkoFlowWrapper, or the http/1.1 tracer chose not to start a span
  return handler.apply(serverRequest);
}
```

That also avoids the wrapper starting http/1.1 spans that `PekkoHttpServerTracer` intentionally didn't start.

